cceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"It is an estimate of annual federal tax liability and take-home pay under the listed assumptions. It uses the standard deduction and does not calculate an employer's exact withholding under IRS Publication 15-T. State tax, credits and health premiums are also left out. Compare the per-check result with a pay stub if you need to understand a difference."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I calculate my paycheck manually?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Start with annual gross pay and subtract pre-tax retirement contributions and the standard deduction to find taxable income. Apply the federal brackets to taxable income band by band. Then subtract federal income tax, Social Security, Medicare and the retirement contribution from gross pay. Divide annual take-home pay by the number of checks in your chosen pay schedule."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does this paycheck calculator include state tax?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. The paycheck calculator does not subtract state or local income tax. Its take-home figure is therefore before those taxes. If state or local tax appears on your pay stub, that is one reason the actual net check can be lower than this estimate."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Why is my real paycheck different from this estimate?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Your employer's withholding can differ from this annual-liability estimate. The calculator also leaves out state tax, credits, health premiums and extra W-4 withholding. It does not model tips or overtime deductions. Compare the deduction rows with your pay stub to identify which amounts the estimate leaves out."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How does a 401(k) contribution change my take-home pay?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A pre-tax retirement contribution reduces the income used for federal income tax, while Social Security and Medicare still use gross wages. In the $100,000 single-filer case, a $10,000 contribution lowers federal income tax by $2,200. Take-home pay falls by $7,800 rather than the full contribution amount. Enter your annual contribution to see the change in the calculator."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Why does Social Security tax stop on high salaries?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Social Security tax applies only up to the 2026 wage base of $184,500. The employee rate is 6.2%, so the modeled Social Security tax on a $300,000 salary is $11,439. Medicare still applies to gross wages above the Social Security wage base. The calculator shows both taxes separately in its results."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does a paycheck calculator include Medicare tax?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. This paycheck calculator subtracts Medicare tax at 1.45% of gross wages. It also models an extra 0.9% withheld on wages above $200,000. Final Additional Medicare liability can use a different threshold for married filing jointly, so that withholding estimate may differ from the final tax."}}]}</script><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Person","name":"Jonathan Velez","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com/about/","jobTitle":"Personal Finance Writer & Editor","description":"Jonathan Velez writes and edits ModernWallet's personal-finance content, focused on loans, interest, and debt payoff. Every calculator page is reviewed against primary sources like the CFPB and Federal Reserve.","knowsAbout":["Auto loans","Loan amortization","Interest and APR","Debt payoff strategies","Personal finance"],"worksFor":{"@type":"Organization","name":"ModernWallet","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com"}}</script> <script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Article","headline":"Paycheck Calculator","description":"Estimate take-home pay per paycheck after 2026 federal income tax, Social Security and Medicare. Compare filing statuses and pre-tax retirement contributions.","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com/paycheck-calculator/","image":{"@type":"ImageObject","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com/favicon.svg"},"datePublished":"2026-11-15","dateModified":"2026-11-15","author":{"@type":"Person","name":"Jonathan Velez","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com/about/"},"publisher":{"@type":"Organization","name":"ModernWallet","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com","logo":{"@type":"ImageObject","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com/favicon.svg"}},"citation":[{"@type":"CreativeWork","name":"IRS Revenue Procedure 2025-32 (2026 inflation adjustments)","url":"https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-drop/rp-25-32.pdf"},{"@type":"CreativeWork","name":"IRS: Tax inflation adjustments for tax year 2026","url":"https://www.irs.gov/newsroom/irs-releases-tax-inflation-adjustments-for-tax-year-2026-including-amendments-from-the-one-big-beautiful-bill"},{"@type":"CreativeWork","name":"IRS Topic no. 751, Social Security and Medicare withholding rates","url":"https://www.irs.gov/taxtopics/tc751"},{"@type":"CreativeWork","name":"IRS Topic no. 560, Additional Medicare Tax","url":"https://www.irs.gov/taxtopics/tc560"}]}</script> <main class="page" data-astro-cid-5nhdsx2r> <nav class="crumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb" data-astro-cid-5nhdsx2r> <a href="/" data-astro-cid-5nhdsx2r>ModernWallet</a> <span data-astro-cid-5nhdsx2r>/</span> <span class="crumb-current" data-astro-cid-5nhdsx2r>Paycheck</span> </nav> <header class="hero" data-astro-cid-5nhdsx2r> <h1 data-astro-cid-5nhdsx2r>Paycheck Calculator</h1> <div class="byline" data-astro-cid-oxbng3yr> <span class="by" data-astro-cid-oxbng3yr>By <a href="/about/" data-astro-cid-oxbng3yr>Jonathan Velez</a>, Personal Finance Writer &amp; Editor</span> <span class="sep" aria-hidden="true" data-astro-cid-oxbng3yr>·</span> <span class="rev" data-astro-cid-oxbng3yr>Reviewed against primary sources · Updated November 15, 2026</span> </div>   <p class="lede" data-astro-cid-5nhdsx2r>At $60,000, a single filer keeps an estimated $50,390 a year before state tax. That is $1,938.08 per biweekly paycheck after federal income tax and payroll tax. The paycheck calculator above starts with annual gross pay and biweekly checks, but you can enter pay per paycheck and choose a different schedule.</p><p class="lede" data-astro-cid-5nhdsx2r>It shows take-home pay per check as the headline result, with each deduction shown per check and per year. The result estimates annual federal tax liability for a W-2 employee who takes the standard deduction. It does not predict the exact amount an employer will withhold.</p><p class="lede" data-astro-cid-5nhdsx2r>For the worked example, enter $60,000 in annual gross pay, choose single filing and leave pre-tax retirement at $0. The standard deduction leaves $43,900 of taxable income. Federal income tax comes to $5,020, while Social Security takes $3,720 and Medicare takes $870.</p><p class="lede" data-astro-cid-5nhdsx2r>Together, those payroll taxes are $4,590. Subtract federal and payroll tax from gross pay to get $50,390 for the year. Across 26 biweekly checks, that is $1,938.08 per check, or 84.0% of gross pay.</p><p class="lede" data-astro-cid-5nhdsx2r>State tax, credits and other withholding could change what appears on a pay stub.</p> </header> <div class="tool-wrap" data-astro-cid-5nhdsx2r> <button type="button" class="share-btn" data-share-title="Paycheck Calculator" aria-label="Share this tool" data-astro-cid-qan2qojn> <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="16" height="16" aria-hidden="true" data-astro-cid-qan2qojn><path d="M12 3v12M7.5 7.5 12 3l4.5 4.5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" data-astro-cid-qan2qojn></path><path d="M5 12v7a1 1 0 0 0 1 1h12a1 1 0 0 0 1-1v-7" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" data-astro-cid-qan2qojn></path></svg> <span class="share-label" data-astro-cid-qan2qojn>Share</span> </button> <span class="share-stat